# self-healing loop crontab for user `joblander` (JOB-731 Phase 2).
# Installed WHOLESALE by init/init.sh: `crontab -u joblander <this file>` —
# it owns the entire crontab; hand edits on the VM are lost on re-init.
#
# Legacy reference: deploy/cron/joblander-agents.crontab.snapshot (old VM, as-found).

# The TS watcher's notify.sh lives in the workspace clone on the NEW VM layout
# (default inside the watcher still points at the legacy /home/joblander/joblander/... path).
WATCH_NOTIFY_SCRIPT=/home/joblander/workspace/scripts/notify.sh

# Consecutive bad samples (= minutes) before the watcher pages. Default in
# watcher/src/config.ts is 3; measured evidence says 3 is far too low.
#
# 12 days of per-minute heartbeats (17,254 samples, /home/joblander/output-watch.log),
# last 7 days: 36 bad episodes — 7 crossed the threshold and paged, 29 died before it.
#   paged-episode duration : min 3m / median 4m / MAX 13m
#   page -> recovery       : min 1m / median 2m / MAX 11m
# All 7 pages were investigated by the dispatcher and ALL 7 were false positives
# (JOB-796/798/806/807/809/814/854 — every one closed not-a-bug or stale). Precision
# of this pager over the week was zero, at ~$8 of investigation cost.
#
# No bad episode in the whole 12-day log lasted longer than 13 minutes, and every
# single one self-resolved. A 15-minute threshold therefore suppresses all 36
# episodes — including all 7 pages — while costing at most 15 minutes of detection
# delay on a real outage. The failure class this pager exists for (JOB-651: silent
# total STT outage) ran 19 HOURS, so 15 minutes is noise against it.
#
# This is a mitigation, not the fix. The root cause is that /health/output cannot
# distinguish "audio present but nobody is being interviewed" from "pipeline dead" —
# both produce zero hints. backend#332 fixed the gate-suppression class (1 of the 7);
# the remaining 4 need a transcript-stage discriminator. Revisit this value once
# that lands.
WATCH_THRESHOLD=15

# Watcher tick (JOB-670): product-output pager, every minute.
# The `&& gcloud logging write ...` chain ships the heartbeat to Cloud Logging
# ONLY on exit 0 — a crashed tick writes no heartbeat, which is exactly what
# the Terraform dead-man alert (WATCHER_HEARTBEAT absent 5 min) catches.
# Log id `self-healing-watcher` must match var.heartbeat_log_id in
# infra/terraform/modules/self-healing-vm/variables.tf.
#
# --- ROLE HANDED OVER (TEST-PLAN stage 3, 2026-07-16): this VM is the LIVE
# pager; the legacy VM's watcher cron line is commented out. Stage 4 (2026-07-17):
# trigger targets THIS VM's dispatcher (default localhost:4100) — full loop local.
* * * * * cd /home/joblander/self-healing/watcher && /usr/bin/node dist/tick.js >> /home/joblander/output-watch.log 2>&1 && touch /home/joblander/.watcher-last-ok && gcloud logging write self-healing-watcher "WATCHER_HEARTBEAT tick ok" --severity=INFO >/dev/null 2>&1

# The `touch` above is a LOCAL heartbeat and lands BEFORE the Cloud Logging
# write on purpose. On 2026-08-26 the tick kept succeeding every minute for six
# days while `gcloud logging write` failed silently — the VM had lost its DHCP
# address, so the only heartbeat the system had needed the very thing that was
# broken. The file mtime tells a later investigator "the watcher was alive, the
# network was not", which is the difference between a 5-minute fix and a
# six-day outage. selfheal-netwatch.timer is what actually repairs it.

# Hourly monitor session (error-side triage; runs from the workspace clone —
# run-monitor-session.sh sources scripts/lib/agent-launcher.sh next to it).
# --- ROLE HANDED OVER (stage 3M, 2026-07-17): this VM runs the hourly monitor;
# the legacy VM's hourly cron is commented out. State (teams/logs/monitoring)
# was copied from the legacy VM before handover. Runs from THIS repo.
0 * * * * systemd-cat -t joblander-monitor /home/joblander/self-healing/monitor/run-monitor-session.sh

# --- CWS crons: ENABLED AT CUTOVER (Phase 4) ---------------------------------
# The legacy joblander-agents VM still runs these; enabling here too would
# double-run them. Uncomment only on the owner's cutover signal.
# 0 8 * * * /home/joblander/cws-recon/run.sh >> /home/joblander/cws-recon/cron.log 2>&1
# 5 8 * * 1 bash /home/joblander/workspace/scripts/run-cws-metrics.sh >> /home/joblander/workspace/reports/cws-rankings/cron.log 2>&1

# Dispatcher liveness → Cloud Logging, every 5 min. The watcher heartbeat only
# proves the VM and cron are alive; the dispatcher could be dead (crash-looped,
# hit its cgroup ceiling, wedged mid-run) with the heartbeat still ticking, and
# nothing outside the box would know. The off-box watchdog alerts on the
# absence of this line — see infra/terraform/modules/self-healing-watchdog.
*/5 * * * * curl -sf --max-time 10 http://localhost:4100/health 2>/dev/null | grep -q '"status":"ok"' && gcloud logging write self-healing-dispatcher "DISPATCHER_HEARTBEAT ok" --severity=INFO >/dev/null 2>&1
